fication.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-73137" "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\left({\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}\\frac{2+k}{1+k}\\right)"] "category" "local_authored" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653}] "sources" [{"question" "q-73137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/assets/99f224712b0a067d76bc02c1f33938446ad84883a439ba53cb6569197e2cf9fd.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{x}$ between $x=1$, $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=4,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3-1}f(1+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}f(1+k) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}\\frac{1+k+1}{1+k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}\\frac{2+k}{1+k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-73137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}\\frac{2+k}{1+k}"] ["math" "{\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}\\frac{k}{1+k}"] ["math" "{\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{1+k}{2+k}"] ["math" "{\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{3+k}{2+k}"] ["math" "{\\sum }_{k=1}^{4}\\frac{2+k}{k}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73137" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}\\frac{2+k}{1+k}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+8)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{5}{n}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡($\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=3,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-2+\\frac{5j}{n})+8) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{20j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-345034" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345034" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{20j}{n}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-315717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3/2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $10$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j.$*\n\n${S}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n ${\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}($\n{{field-1}}$)^{3/2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-315717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're given that there are  $n=10$ subintervals.  The endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=3,$  so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{10}=\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{10-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}f(\\frac{2j-4}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}{((-2+\\frac{j}{2})+2)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}{(\\frac{j}{2})}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-315717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-315717" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-315717" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{j}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/assets/e81e7d9d60e38c52f86c5eee784ebcfdeb91d4445b6539cc01a6ac9f5ededa89.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=\\frac{6}{x}$ between $x=1$, $x=6$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $k.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{k=0}^{4}$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=5$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=6,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-1}{5}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{5-1}f(1+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{4}f(1+k) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{4}\\frac{6}{1+k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-345027" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345027" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{1+k}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-315701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-4)$ between $x=2$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡($\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-315701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=2$ and $b=5,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(2+\\frac{3j}{n})-4) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-315701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-315701" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-315701" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6j}{n}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3)$ between $x=1$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡($\n{{field-1}} $)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(1+\\frac{2j}{n})-3)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\frac{8j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-345048" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345048" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8j}{n}+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/assets/f321b063e3bc76eeb5b426d00cbf94451a06cec928008eaadc965547691c412a.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=4+x$ between $x=0,$ $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a right Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n${S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=4$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=4,$ so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-0}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(0+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}(4+i).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-345052" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345052" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4+i"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y={e}^{x}$ between $x=1$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is  \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$  with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(1+\\frac{4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{e}^{(1+\\frac{4j}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{e}^{1}\\cdot {e}^{4j/n} \\\\  & =\\frac{4e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt[n]{{e}^{4j}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-73208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{4e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt[n]{{e}^{4j}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt[n]{{e}^{j}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt{{e}^{j}}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt[n]{{e}^{j}}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt{{e}^{j}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt[n]{{e}^{4j}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+8}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $8$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n${S}_{R}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is   \n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n=8$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=2.$ So, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-2)}{8}=\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(-2+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}f(-2+\\frac{i}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}\\,\\sqrt{4\\,(-2+\\frac{i}{2})+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}\\,\\sqrt{2i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-345037" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-345037" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ between $x=1$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(\\frac{n+4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{n+4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc8f4454c7de2798b87b3e48158a8f49815f992da1504fcfa6cfaebfcb5126d"} {"question" "q-73192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{4n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+4j)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4j)"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)]"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4j)"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "q-73192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n+4j)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/state.json" "file_sha256" "2cf848a7d49b9b9bdee6211b328aa8a534a5b2ab12d7e1b0a95ac0c6f5182eb4"} {"question" "e-401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y=4-x^2$ between $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown below. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/assets/b517da6e730ac50341b133a60ae8ab99cfed7b2ce92638e6028bb3fac6fbf9da.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/example-401.json" "file_sha256" "494ce76718574d6fd87a036ac946075163d1a8aab7fa06987354ce6e8b4c965a"} {"question" "e-401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ with $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=2,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-(-1)}{3} = \\dfrac 3 3 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3-1}f(-1+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}f(k-1) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(4-(k-1)^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(4-{k}^{2}+2k-1) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(3+2k-{k}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/example-401.json" "file_sha256" "494ce76718574d6fd87a036ac946075163d1a8aab7fa06987354ce6e8b4c965a"} {"question" "e-402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The area under the curve $y=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{ x}{2}\\right)$ between $x=0$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $3$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/example-402.json" "file_sha256" "186db9726be7695ac974adec6408fdfaeb98afc75e278ced2132c9a270e86ce5"} {"question" "e-402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_i = a + i\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nNote that here we are using the index $i$ instead of $k.$ It doesn't matter which letter we use for the counter.\n\nWe're told that there are $n=3$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3} = \\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(0+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{i}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079618/example-402.json" "file_sha256" "186db9726be7695ac974adec6408fdfaeb98afc75e278ced2132c9a270e86ce5"}] "usage" {"q-73208" [] "e-402" [] "e-401" [] "q-73137" [] "q-345034" []} "basis" 2822 "retractions" [[17592186261464 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/99f224712b0a067d76bc02c1f33938446ad84883a439ba53cb6569197e2cf9fd.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=\\frac{x+1}{x}$ between $x=1$, $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?"] [17592186261464 :question/answer-fields 17592186274957] [17592186260055 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}(4x+8)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{5}{n}\\,\\operatorname{sin}($\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [17592186260055 :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=3,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(4(-2+\\frac{5j}{n})+8) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{20j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186261487 :question/answer-fields 17592186274964] [17592186096351 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=4-x^2$ between $x=-1$, $x=2$, and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown below. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1042/Source/Images/ed16173ebe0275016a94e29e27948e67.png)"] [17592186096351 :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=2$, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-(-1)}{3} = \\dfrac 3 3 = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)f(x_{k})Δx &= ∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)f(a + kΔx)Δx \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(3 - 1)f(-1 + k \\cdot 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(2)f(k - 1) \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(2)(4 - (k - 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(2)(4 - k^{2} + 2k - 1) \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(2)(3 + 2k - k^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186096352 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)$ between $x=0$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $3$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?"] [17592186096352 :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i)\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx_i = a + i\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nNote that here we are using the index $i$ instead of $k$. It doesn't matter which letter we use for the counter.\n\nWe're told that there are $n=3$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3} = \\dfrac{3}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i)\\Delta x &= \\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(a + i\\Delta x)\\Delta x \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i=1}^{3} f(0 + i \\cdot 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i=1}^{3} f(i) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i=1}^{3} \\cos\\left(\\frac{i}{2}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186260057 17592186273367 17592186273368 17592186273369 17592186273370 17592186273371 17592186273402 17592186273403 17592186273404 17592186273405 17592186273406] "immutable_fields" [[17592186260056 :answer-field/correct] [17592186260056 :answer-field/id] [17592186260056 :answer-field/key] [17592186260056 :answer-field/type] [17592186274957 :answer-field/correct] [17592186274957 :answer-field/id] [17592186274957 :answer-field/key] [17592186274957 :answer-field/type] [17592186274964 :answer-field/correct] [17592186274964 :answer-field/id] [17592186274964 :answer-field/key] [17592186274964 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
